(window.guideContent=window.guideContent||[]).push(["memory",{"l:memory/short-term-memory-how-chat-history-really-works":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A model has no memory between calls. What feels like memory in a chat is the history your application, or the provider, sends again on every turn. Stateful API options store that history on the server for you, but the model still reads, and you still pay for, all of it on every turn.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The turn loop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()  # or OpenAI(base_url=..., api_key=...) for any OpenAI-compatible provider\nhistory = [{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You are Atlas, a concise assistant.\"}]\n\ndef chat(user_text: str) -&gt; str:\n    history.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": user_text})\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", messages=history)\n    reply = r.choices[0].message.content\n    history.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": reply})\n    print(r.usage.prompt_tokens, \"input tokens this turn\")\n    return reply\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Turn 1 sends two messages; turn 10 sends twenty. Remove the two \u003ccode>history.append\u003c/code> lines and the model forgets everything, because nothing else carries the conversation from one call to the next.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>What the model actually sees\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The API turns your message list (and any tool schemas) into one long token sequence using the model's \u003cb>chat template\u003c/b>. For an open-weight model with a ChatML-style template, three turns render roughly like this:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>&lt;|im_start|&gt;system\nYou are Atlas, a concise assistant.&lt;|im_end|&gt;\n&lt;|im_start|&gt;user\nMy name is Priya and I work on billing.&lt;|im_end|&gt;\n&lt;|im_start|&gt;assistant\nNice to meet you, Priya. What do you need?&lt;|im_end|&gt;\n&lt;|im_start|&gt;user\nWhat's my name?&lt;|im_end|&gt;\n&lt;|im_start|&gt;assistant\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The model \"remembers\" Priya only because the earlier turn is in this text. Hosted APIs do the same rendering on their servers, sometimes adding hidden instructions of their own. In an agent, the sequence also contains every tool call and tool result. With reasoning models it may contain earlier reasoning too: recent Claude models (Opus 4.5 and later, for example) keep earlier thinking blocks by default and bill them as input, and OpenAI's Responses API returns encrypted reasoning items that you pass back in stateless mode so the model can build on its earlier reasoning.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Stateless and stateful options\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>Who stores the history\u003c/th>\u003cth>What you send each turn\u003c/th>\u003cth>What is billed\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Chat Completions, Anthropic Messages, most open-model servers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>You\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The full message list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All input tokens, every turn\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Responses with \u003ccode>previous_response_id\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI (responses kept 30 days by default; \u003ccode>store=false\u003c/code> turns this off)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The new message plus the previous response id\u003c/td>\u003ctd>All previous input tokens in the chain, as input\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>OpenAI Conversations API\u003c/td>\u003ctd>OpenAI, as a durable conversation object without the 30-day limit\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The new message plus the conversation id\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The history the model reads, as input\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Framework threads (LangGraph checkpointers, agent SDK sessions)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your database, managed by the framework\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The framework rebuilds the list\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Same as stateless\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Stateful APIs save you code and bandwidth, not tokens. They also hand the history to the provider, which matters for data retention and for any history management you want to control yourself.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Where history lives in production\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Keep your own canonical log even if you use a stateful API:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>messages(conversation_id, seq, role, content_json, token_count, created_at, model, request_id)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Raw log\u003c/b>\u003csmall>every message, kept\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>View builder\u003c/b>\u003csmall>trim, compact, clear old tool results\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Context assembler\u003c/b>\u003csmall>budgets, order\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Model request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The list you send to the model is a \u003cb>view\u003c/b> derived from this log: possibly trimmed, compacted or with old tool results cleared. The raw log lets you re-compact later with a better prompt, build evals from real conversations, honour deletion requests and debug exactly what the model saw.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Orphaned tool calls.\u003c/b> Dropping a tool result but keeping its call (or the reverse) makes most APIs reject the request. Trim in pairs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Editing earlier messages in place.\u003c/b> It breaks prompt caches (lesson 5) and, on some models, invalidates signed reasoning blocks.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Shared state between users.\u003c/b> A global \u003ccode>history\u003c/code> list in a web server leaks one user's chat into another's. Key everything by conversation and user.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Losing the system prompt\u003c/b> when trimming. It must be sent on every call.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">The provider stores your conversation. Why is the bill for turn 30 still much larger than for turn 1?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The model itself is stateless: the provider re-reads the whole stored history as input on every turn, and those tokens are billed (OpenAI states that all previous input tokens in a previous_response_id chain are billed as input). Server-side storage changes where the history lives, not what the model reads.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why keep a raw message log if you send a compacted view to the model?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The raw log is the source of truth for debugging, audits, re-compacting with a better prompt, building evals and honouring deletion requests. The compacted view is lossy and can always be rebuilt from the log, but not the other way round.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What can break if you trim history by deleting the oldest message without care?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>You can orphan a tool call from its result (an API error), drop the system prompt or the message that defined the task, and you change the prefix, so the prompt cache misses on the next request.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:memory/history-strategies":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Five common strategies decide which past turns go into the next request: send everything, keep the last N turns, keep as many recent turns as fit a token cap, keep a running summary plus recent turns, or a hybrid that adds pinned facts and retrieval over old turns. They trade cost and focus against recall of old details, and they behave very differently with prompt caching.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The five strategies\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\u003cth>What is sent\u003c/th>\u003cth>Strengths\u003c/th>\u003cth>Weaknesses\u003c/th>\u003cth>Use when\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Full history\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Everything\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Perfect recall; append-only, so cache-friendly\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Grows without limit: cost, latency, context rot, eventual overflow\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Short sessions; tasks that need exact earlier detail\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sliding window\u003c/td>\u003ctd>System prompt plus the last N turns\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Simple and bounded\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Forgets early facts abruptly, including the task itself; the prefix shifts every turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Casual chat where old turns do not matter\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Token trimming\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Newest turns until a token cap\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bounded by what actually costs (tokens)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Same forgetting; needs counting\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Turns of very different lengths; tool-heavy agents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Summary buffer\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A running summary of older turns plus the last K turns verbatim\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Bounded, keeps the gist\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Summaries lose detail; extra model calls; summary errors persist\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long chats and support sessions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Hybrid\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Pinned facts, summary, recent window, retrieval over old turns, cleared tool results\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Best recall per token\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most moving parts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Production assistants and long-running agents\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The code\u003c/h4>\u003cp>First, group messages into \u003cb>turns\u003c/b> that start with a real user message, so a tool call always travels with its result. (In Anthropic's format tool results arrive as user messages, so they must not start a new turn.)\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def n_tok(msg: dict) -&gt; int:\n    return len(str(msg.get(\"content\", \"\"))) // 4 + 4  # estimate; use your provider's counter\n\ndef is_new_turn(m: dict) -&gt; bool:\n    if m[\"role\"] != \"user\":\n        return False\n    c = m.get(\"content\")\n    return not (isinstance(c, list) and any(b.get(\"type\") == \"tool_result\" for b in c))\n\ndef turns(history: list[dict]) -&gt; list[list[dict]]:\n    groups = []\n    for m in history:\n        if is_new_turn(m) or not groups:\n            groups.append([])\n        groups[-1].append(m)\n    return groups\n\ndef flat(groups):\n    return [m for g in groups for m in g]\n\ndef full(system, history):\n    return [system, *history]\n\ndef window(system, history, n_turns=8):\n    return [system, *flat(turns(history)[-n_turns:])]\n\ndef token_trim(system, history, max_tokens=12_000):\n    kept, used = [], 0\n    for g in reversed(turns(history)):\n        t = sum(n_tok(m) for m in g)\n        if used + t &gt; max_tokens:\n            break\n        kept.insert(0, g)\n        used += t\n    return [system, *flat(kept)]\n\ndef summary_buffer(system, history, state, keep_turns=4, trigger_tokens=20_000):\n    \"\"\"state = {\"summary\": \"\", \"upto\": 0}; 'upto' counts turns already folded into the summary.\"\"\"\n    gs = turns(history)\n    live = gs[state[\"upto\"]:]\n    if sum(n_tok(m) for m in flat(live)) &gt; trigger_tokens and len(live) &gt; keep_turns:\n        fold = live[:-keep_turns]\n        state[\"summary\"] = summarise(state[\"summary\"], flat(fold))  # one model call, next lesson\n        state[\"upto\"] += len(fold)\n    head = [system]\n    if state[\"summary\"]:\n        head.append({\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Summary of the earlier conversation:\\n\" + state[\"summary\"]})\n    return [*head, *flat(gs[state[\"upto\"]:])]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two design points hide in \u003ccode>summary_buffer\u003c/code>. It folds turns in \u003cb>large batches\u003c/b> when a threshold is crossed, not one turn at a time, and the summary text then stays fixed until the next fold. Both choices keep the prefix stable, which matters for caching (lesson 5). With Anthropic's API, put the summary in a second \u003ccode>system\u003c/code> block instead of a system message.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Worked numbers\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A 40-turn chat with a 1,000-token system prompt, where each turn adds 600 tokens (200 from the user, 400 from the assistant):\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\u003cth>Largest request\u003c/th>\u003cth>Input tokens over 40 turns\u003c/th>\u003cth>Extra model calls\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Full history\u003c/td>\u003ctd>24,600\u003c/td>\u003ctd>516,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Window of 8 turns\u003c/td>\u003ctd>6,000\u003c/td>\u003ctd>218,400\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Summary buffer (fold when unsummarised history exceeds 6,000; keep 4 turns; 500-token summary)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>7,700\u003c/td>\u003ctd>215,500\u003c/td>\u003ctd>5 summaries\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>On tokens alone the window and the summary buffer look alike, but the window has forgotten everything from before turn 32 while the summary buffer kept the gist. And token totals are not the bill: lesson 5 shows how caching changes the ranking.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A common default as of late 2026:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>Full history until the conversation reaches a threshold, for example 60 to 70% of your working budget.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Then compact: summarise the older turns and keep the last few verbatim (lesson 3), using your own summariser or the provider's server-side compaction.\u003c/li>\u003cli>In agents, clear old tool results first, because they are the bulk (lesson 4).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Pin a few facts outside the window, such as the user's name and the task goal, and move cross-session facts into long-term memory (lessons 6 to 9).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cp>Frameworks package these steps. LangChain's \u003ccode>SummarizationMiddleware\u003c/code>, for example, takes a trigger and a keep rule (its docs show \u003ccode>trigger=(\"tokens\", 4000)\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>keep=(\"messages\", 20)\u003c/code>), and agent SDKs offer session objects that persist history. Whatever you use, check three rules: never split a tool exchange, always start the kept window on a user turn, and keep the first user message if it defines the task.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A user gives their name in turn 1. At turn 25 your assistant, which uses an 8-turn window, cannot remember it. Give two fixes.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Switch to a summary buffer or compaction so early facts survive in the summary; pin key facts (name, goal) in a block outside the window; or extract the name into long-term memory and load the user profile each turn.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why group messages into turns before trimming?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>So a tool call is never separated from its result (which APIs reject) and the kept window always starts on a real user message, giving the model a coherent conversation.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When is full history the right choice?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When sessions are short enough to stay well within the working budget, or when exact recall of earlier detail matters more than cost; it is also the most cache-friendly strategy, because history is append-only.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:compaction-and-summarisation-of-history":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Compaction\u003c/b> replaces older turns with a model-written summary, so a conversation or agent can continue past the window and stay focused. The craft is deciding what the summary must keep: goals, decisions, current state, open tasks and exact identifiers. As of late 2026 Anthropic and OpenAI both offer server-side compaction; you can also run your own summariser on any model.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"flow\" role=\"img\" aria-label=\"Diagram\">\u003cdiv class=\"fr\">\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Turns 1 to 20\u003c/b>\u003csmall>21k tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Summary call\u003c/b>\u003csmall>same or cheaper model\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Summary\u003c/b>\u003csmall>2k tokens\u003c/small>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Summary plus turns 21 to 24 verbatim\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fa\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&rarr;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"fn\">\u003cb>Next request\u003c/b>\u003c/span>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What to keep and what to drop\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Keep, verbatim where possible\u003c/th>\u003cth>Drop or shrink\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>The task, success criteria and constraints the user set\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Raw tool outputs already used (keep the conclusion and a pointer)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Decisions made, and why\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Superseded drafts and abandoned plans (one line on why)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Current state: what is done, files and records changed, artefacts\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Greetings, thanks and small talk\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Open tasks, next steps and blockers\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Repeated questions and restated content\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Exact identifiers: paths, ids, names, numbers, URLs, error messages\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long reasoning that led to a recorded decision\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Errors hit and approaches that failed\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Full file contents (they can be re-read)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>User preferences and promises made\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Old search results and directory listings\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Anthropic's advice for tuning a compaction prompt: run it on complex real traces, first maximise recall so nothing relevant is lost, then improve precision by cutting what is superfluous. Its default summary prompt is structured the same way: task overview, current state, important discoveries (including failed approaches), next steps, and context to preserve such as preferences and promises.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>A compaction prompt\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>You are compacting a conversation so the work can continue in a fresh context window.\nWrite a summary inside &lt;summary&gt;&lt;/summary&gt; with these sections:\n1. Goal: the user's request, success criteria and constraints, in their words where possible.\n2. State: what is done; files, records or artefacts created or changed, with exact paths and ids.\n3. Decisions: each decision and its reason.\n4. Open items: next steps in priority order, blockers, unanswered questions.\n5. Facts to preserve: names, numbers, ids, URLs, error messages, user preferences, promises made.\n6. Dead ends: approaches that failed, and why.\nBe concise but complete. Copy identifiers exactly. Do not add anything that is not in the transcript.\nDo not call any tools; respond with text only.\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The last line matters: when tools are defined, a model asked to summarise sometimes calls a tool instead of writing the summary, and Anthropic's docs recommend forbidding it explicitly.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Your own compaction, on any provider\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def compact(client, model, history, keep_turns=4):\n    gs = turns(history)  # from the History strategies lesson\n    old, recent = flat(gs[:-keep_turns]), flat(gs[-keep_turns:])\n    if not old:\n        return history\n    transcript = \"\\n\".join(f\"{m['role']}: {m.get('content')}\" for m in old)\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(\n        model=model, max_tokens=3000,\n        messages=[{\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": COMPACTION_PROMPT},\n                  {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": transcript}])\n    summary = r.choices[0].message.content\n    return [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"Summary of our work so far:\\n\" + summary},\n            {\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": \"Understood. I will continue from this summary.\"},\n            *recent]\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Trigger it between turns, never while a tool call is waiting for its result, when the last request's input (including cached tokens) passes your threshold. Because the summary is generated by a separate call, you can use a cheaper model for it and run it in the background.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Server-side compaction (late 2026)\u003c/h4>\u003cp>\u003cb>Anthropic\u003c/b> offers two beta modes on the Messages API:\u003c/p>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003ci>Compaction on demand\u003c/i> (beta header \u003ccode>compact-2026-09-04\u003c/code>). You send the conversation with \u003ccode>compaction={\"type\": \"summarize\"}\u003c/code>. The response contains one \u003ccode>compaction\u003c/code> block (a readable summary plus a signature) and \u003ccode>stop_reason\u003c/code> \u003ccode>\"compaction\"\u003c/code>. You replace the summarised messages with that block, first in \u003ccode>messages\u003c/code>, and send it unchanged, with the beta header, on every later request. To keep recent turns word for word, compact only the messages before your cut point and send the kept turns after the block. Optional \u003ccode>instructions\u003c/code> (up to 16,384 characters) replace the default summary prompt. It can run in the background while the conversation continues.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003ci>Compaction at a token threshold\u003c/i> (beta header \u003ccode>compact-2026-01-12\u003c/code>). You add \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"compact_20260112\", \"trigger\": {\"type\": \"input_tokens\", \"value\": 150000}}\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>context_management.edits\u003c/code> (150,000 is the default; the minimum is 50,000). When input reaches the trigger, the API summarises inside that request, returns a \u003ccode>compaction\u003c/code> block at the start of the response and carries on; on later requests it ignores everything before the block. \u003ccode>pause_after_compaction\u003c/code> lets you re-insert recent turns before it continues.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import anthropic\n\nclient = anthropic.Anthropic()\n\ndef maybe_compact(history, system, usage, limit=120_000):\n    used = (usage.input_tokens + usage.cache_read_input_tokens\n            + usage.cache_creation_input_tokens + usage.output_tokens)\n    if used &lt; limit:\n        return history\n    r = client.beta.messages.create(\n        model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", max_tokens=4096, system=system, messages=history,\n        betas=[\"compact-2026-09-04\"], compaction={\"type\": \"summarize\"})\n    if r.stop_reason != \"compaction\":  # e.g. max_tokens or refusal: keep the history, try later\n        return history\n    return [{\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": r.content}]  # the signed block replaces the old turns\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In both modes the summary is written by the model you call, the summarisation step is billed and rate-limited like any request, and the cost is reported in \u003ccode>usage.iterations\u003c/code>, so sum that array when you track spend.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>OpenAI\u003c/b> offers compaction in the Responses API. Add \u003ccode>context_management=[{\"type\": \"compaction\", \"compact_threshold\": 200000}]\u003c/code> to a normal request; when the rendered context crosses the threshold, the server compacts, emits a compaction item in the output and continues. The item is encrypted and not human-readable. With stateless chaining you append the output items as usual (and may drop items before the latest compaction item); with \u003ccode>previous_response_id\u003c/code> you send only the new message and must not prune. It works with \u003ccode>store=False\u003c/code> for zero data retention. For explicit control, \u003ccode>client.responses.compact(model=..., input=items)\u003c/code> returns a new, shorter window that you pass as-is into the next call.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>response = client.responses.create(\n    model=\"&lt;model&gt;\", input=conversation, store=False,\n    context_management=[{\"type\": \"compaction\", \"compact_threshold\": 200_000}])\nconversation.extend(response.output)  # includes the compaction item if one was made\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Coding agents compact for you\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Claude Code compacts automatically as the window fills. You can steer it with \u003ccode>/compact focus on the auth bug\u003c/code>, compact earlier, or \u003ccode>/clear\u003c/code> between unrelated tasks. After compaction it re-reads up to five recently modified files and re-injects the project CLAUDE.md, auto memory and any plan, but instructions you gave only in chat survive only as well as the summary keeps them. Codex CLI has a \u003ccode>/compact\u003c/code> command too. The lesson for your own agents: re-inject durable instructions after every compaction, and keep important state in files (lesson 8), not only in the conversation.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Summarise old turns and compare quality with full history: how would you run that comparison, and what do you expect?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Take about 20 long real conversations and write three probe questions per conversation about early details (a number, a decision, a stated preference). Answer each probe twice, once with the full history and once with the compacted history (summary plus the last few turns), and grade with a rubric or an LLM judge, recording input tokens for both. Expect near-parity on goals and decisions and losses on exact numbers and identifiers unless the compaction prompt asks for them; iterate on the prompt until the probe scores match, typically at several times fewer input tokens.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What must a coding agent's compaction summary preserve?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The goal and constraints, decisions with reasons, which files changed and the current state, open tasks and next steps, exact paths, commands and error messages, approaches that failed, and any promises made to the user.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">When would you choose Anthropic's on-demand compaction over threshold compaction?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>When your application must control when compaction happens (for example at task boundaries or while the user is idle), cannot pause a turn while a summary is written, or must keep recent turns word for word. Threshold compaction suits a simpler setup where the API manages context inside ordinary requests.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">After compaction an agent redoes work it already finished. What probably went wrong?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The summary dropped the record of completed work, or the result lived only in tool output that was summarised away. Make the prompt list completed steps and changed files explicitly, and keep a progress file outside the conversation that the agent reads after compaction.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:memory/context-editing-clearing-old-tool-results-and-th":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Context editing\u003c/b> removes specific content by rule instead of summarising everything: old tool results (optionally with their inputs) and old thinking blocks. It needs no extra model call, it is predictable, and everything it does not touch stays word for word. But each clearing changes the prefix, so clear in batches big enough to justify rewriting the cache.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Why tool results first\u003c/h4>\u003cp>In agent traces, tool results are usually the largest and least reusable part of the history (Context engineering module, lesson 6). Once the model has read a file and acted on it, the raw content rarely matters again, and if it does, the model can call the tool again. Anthropic describes tool result clearing as one of the safest, lightest-touch forms of compaction.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Anthropic's server-side strategies\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Context editing is a beta feature on the Messages API (header \u003ccode>context-management-2025-06-27\u003c/code>), applied on the server before the prompt reaches the model. Your client keeps the full, unmodified history and keeps sending it; the API decides what to clear.\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Tool result clearing\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>clear_tool_uses_20250919\u003c/code>) clears the oldest tool results first and replaces each with placeholder text telling the model it was removed:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Option\u003c/th>\u003cth>Default\u003c/th>\u003cth>Meaning\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>trigger\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>100,000 input tokens\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Clearing starts once the prompt exceeds this; can also be a number of tool uses\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>keep\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>3 tool uses\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Most recent tool use and result pairs left untouched\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>clear_at_least\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>none\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Minimum tokens to clear each time; if it cannot clear that much, it does nothing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>exclude_tools\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>none\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tools whose results are never cleared\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>clear_tool_inputs\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>false\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Also clear the tool call parameters, not only the results\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>\u003cb>Thinking block clearing\u003c/b> (\u003ccode>clear_thinking_20251015\u003c/code>) controls how many earlier assistant turns keep their thinking blocks: \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"thinking_turns\", \"value\": N}\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>\"all\"\u003c/code>. The default varies by model (recent models keep all of them), so set it explicitly if you run several models. When you combine both strategies, the thinking strategy must come first:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>response = client.beta.messages.create(\n    model=\"&lt;model&gt;\",\n    max_tokens=16000,\n    messages=history,\n    tools=tools,\n    betas=[\"context-management-2025-06-27\"],\n    context_management={\"edits\": [\n        {\"type\": \"clear_thinking_20251015\", \"keep\": {\"type\": \"thinking_turns\", \"value\": 2}},\n        {\"type\": \"clear_tool_uses_20250919\",\n         \"trigger\": {\"type\": \"input_tokens\", \"value\": 50000},\n         \"keep\": {\"type\": \"tool_uses\", \"value\": 5},\n         \"clear_at_least\": {\"type\": \"input_tokens\", \"value\": 10000},\n         \"exclude_tools\": [\"memory\"]},\n    ]},\n)\nprint(response.context_management)  # applied_edits: how many tool uses and tokens were cleared\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The token-counting endpoint accepts the same \u003ccode>context_management\u003c/code> setting and reports both the original and the post-clearing count, so you can preview the effect. As of late 2026 Anthropic presents server-side compaction as the primary strategy for long conversations and context editing as the fine-grained tool for cases like this one.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Clearing and the prompt cache\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Tool result clearing invalidates the cached prefix\u003c/b> from the first cleared result onward. That is why \u003ccode>clear_at_least\u003c/code> exists: each clearing costs a cache write, so make sure it frees enough tokens to pay for it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Thinking blocks:\u003c/b> keeping them preserves the cache; clearing them invalidates it from the point of clearing. Choose \u003ccode>keep\u003c/code> for cache hits or for room, deliberately.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Pair it with memory\u003c/h4>\u003cp>If the agent also has Anthropic's memory tool (lesson 8), it receives an automatic warning as the context approaches the clearing threshold, so it can write anything important from those tool results to its memory files before they disappear. Exclude the memory tool itself from clearing.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Do it yourself on any provider\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The same idea in a few lines for OpenAI-style messages. It is applied to the \u003ci>view\u003c/i> sent to the model; the raw log keeps everything. The \u003ccode>cleared\u003c/code> set is stored with the conversation, so a result stays cleared on every later request and the prefix stays stable between batches.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def apply_clearing(view: list[dict], cleared: set) -&gt; list[dict]:\n    calls = {c[\"id\"]: c[\"function\"] for m in view for c in (m.get(\"tool_calls\") or [])}\n    out = []\n    for m in view:\n        if m[\"role\"] == \"tool\" and m[\"tool_call_id\"] in cleared:\n            f = calls.get(m[\"tool_call_id\"], {\"name\": \"the tool\", \"arguments\": \"\"})\n            m = {**m, \"content\": f\"[Cleared: output of {f['name']}({f['arguments']}). Call it again if needed.]\"}\n        out.append(m)\n    return out\n\ndef clear_old_tool_results(view, cleared, keep=3, trigger=60_000, clear_at_least=20_000):\n    out = apply_clearing(view, cleared)\n    if sum(n_tok(m) for m in out) &lt;= trigger:\n        return out\n    results = [m for m in view if m[\"role\"] == \"tool\"]\n    batch = [m for m in results[:-keep] if m[\"tool_call_id\"] not in cleared]\n    if sum(n_tok(m) for m in batch) &gt;= clear_at_least:  # only break the cache for a big win\n        cleared.update(m[\"tool_call_id\"] for m in batch)\n        out = apply_clearing(view, cleared)\n    return out\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The placeholder names the call and its arguments, so the information is \u003ci>restorable\u003c/i>: the model knows exactly how to fetch it again.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Clearing or compaction?\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Context editing\u003c/th>\u003cth>Compaction\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>What goes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Specific blocks, by rule\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Everything before a point, replaced by a summary\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>What can be lost\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Only the cleared items, and they can be re-fetched\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Anything the summary leaves out\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Extra model call\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Yes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Predictability\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Deterministic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Depends on summary quality\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Effect on cache\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rewrite from the first newly cleared item\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rewrite of everything after the stable prefix\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Best for\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tool-heavy agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long conversations and very long tasks\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Most long-running agents use both: clear tool results first because it is cheap and lossless for everything else, and compact when even the cleared history is too large.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why clear tool results before reaching for summarisation?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Tool results are usually the bulk of an agent's history and are rarely needed again in raw form; clearing them needs no model call, leaves the rest of the conversation word for word, and the model can re-run the tool if it needs the data.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does \u003ccode>clear_at_least\u003c/code> protect you from?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Clearing changes the prefix and forces a cache write; without a minimum, the system could clear a tiny amount on every request and pay for a cache rewrite each time. The minimum makes every clearing free enough tokens to be worth it.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Forty steps after clearing, the agent needs the contents of a file it read early on. What happens, and how should you design for it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The result is gone from the context, so the agent must call the tool again. Make that easy: placeholders should say which call produced the result, tools should be cheap to re-run, and anything important should be written to memory or a notes file before it is cleared.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:memory/history-management-and-the-prompt-cache":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prompt caches reuse an exact prefix, so appending to history is cheap and editing it is not: any change invalidates the cache from that point onward. Manage history in rare, large steps at natural boundaries, cache the stable prefix separately so compaction does not throw it away, and judge strategies by total cost and quality, not by token count or hit rate alone. Prices and provider rules are in the Billing module.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What each strategy does to the cache\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\u003cth>What changes in the prefix\u003c/th>\u003cth>Cache effect\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Full history, append-only\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Nothing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Each turn reads everything before it from cache and writes only the new turn\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Sliding window that moves every turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The start of the history, every turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The whole history is rewritten every turn; only tools and system can hit\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Trimming one message at a time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Same\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Same\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Summary rewritten every turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The summary near the top, every turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Same, plus a model call per turn\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Compaction at a threshold\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Once per compaction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One rewrite per compaction, then append-only again\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tool result clearing in batches\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Once per batch, from the first cleared item\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A partial rewrite per batch\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Worked example: 50 turns, three strategies\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Assumptions: tools plus system prompt are 6,000 tokens; each turn adds 1,000 tokens (300 from the user, 700 from the assistant); 50 turns, each sent within the cache lifetime. Illustrative prices: input $3 per million tokens, cache writes 1.25x ($3.75), cache reads 0.1x ($0.30), output $15. Normal answers cost the same output in every strategy, so only input and the compaction summaries are counted.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Strategy\u003c/th>\u003cth>Largest request\u003c/th>\u003cth>Input tokens sent\u003c/th>\u003cth>Input cost for 50 turns\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Full history, no caching\u003c/td>\u003ctd>55,300\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.54M\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$4.62\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Full history, cached\u003c/td>\u003ctd>55,300\u003c/td>\u003ctd>1.54M\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.65\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Window of 8 turns sliding every turn, cached\u003c/td>\u003ctd>14,300\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.68M\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$1.43\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Compaction when history passes 20k (2k summary plus last 4 turns), system cached separately\u003c/td>\u003ctd>26,300\u003c/td>\u003ctd>0.90M\u003c/td>\u003ctd>$0.58, including $0.08 for two summaries\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Look at one turn to see why. At turn 30, full history reads 34,300 tokens from cache ($0.0103) and writes the 1,000 new ones ($0.0038): about \u003cb>$0.014\u003c/b>. The sliding window reads only the 6,000-token system prefix ($0.0018) but must rewrite 8,300 tokens of shifted history at the write price ($0.0311): about \u003cb>$0.033\u003c/b>, more than double, while sending about 60% fewer tokens.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Three lessons from the table:\u003c/p>\u003col>\u003cli>A window that slides every turn is the worst of both worlds: it forgets early facts \u003ci>and\u003c/i> pays write prices on every request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Full history with caching is cheap per turn, but its context keeps growing; past your working budget, quality and latency suffer and the cost per turn keeps climbing.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Compaction in large steps keeps requests bounded and is cheapest overall. The compaction turn itself is expensive (a summary call plus a cache rewrite), so do it rarely.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Rules of thumb\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Cache the stable prefix separately.\u003c/b> On Anthropic, put an explicit breakpoint at the end of the system prompt; then a compaction only writes the new summary, while the system prompt stays cached. Anthropic's compaction docs recommend exactly this. On OpenAI, keep stable content first and, on models that support it, add an explicit breakpoint after it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Use hysteresis.\u003c/b> Trigger at, say, 70% of the working budget and compact down to about 25%, rather than trimming a little every turn.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compact at boundaries:\u003c/b> when a task finishes, in the background while the user reads the last answer, or when the user returns after a pause longer than the cache lifetime. After such a pause the cache is cold anyway, so the rewrite costs nothing extra.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Freeze the summary.\u003c/b> Once written, keep it byte-identical and append new turns after it until the next compaction.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Clear tool results in batches\u003c/b> with a minimum size (\u003ccode>clear_at_least\u003c/code> or your own equivalent).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Keep everything else stable:\u003c/b> tools, model, reasoning settings and output schema; changing them mid-conversation also invalidates the cache.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Measure total cost and quality per conversation.\u003c/b> OpenAI's guide notes that compaction can lower the hit rate of the next request while still reducing total cost; the hit rate alone would mislead you.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>The lifetime matters\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Caches expire. As of late 2026, Anthropic's default lifetime is 5 minutes, refreshed free on every hit, with a 1-hour option at a higher write price; OpenAI's latest models keep entries for at least 30 minutes, with a 12-hour option. If your users usually reply within the lifetime, append-only history and rare compaction pay off. If they come back hours later, every return is a cold start, so compacting aggressively at that moment costs nothing in cache terms.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why can a sliding window cost more than full history with caching, even though it sends fewer tokens?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Moving the window changes the start of the history on every turn, so nothing after the system prefix matches the cache and the whole window is rewritten at the cache-write price each time. Full history is append-only, so almost all of it is read from cache at about a tenth of the input price and only the new turn is written.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Where should a cache breakpoint go so that compaction does not invalidate the system prompt?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At the end of the system prompt (and tools), separate from the conversation. Then the stable prefix keeps hitting the cache after a compaction, and only the summary and recent turns need a new cache write.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A user comes back after two hours. Why is that a good moment to compact?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The cache has expired, so the next request pays full price for the whole history anyway. Compacting first makes that request smaller, and the rewrite of the prefix costs nothing extra in cache terms.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","c:long-term-memory-for-assistants-and-agents":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>\u003cb>Long-term memory\u003c/b> is information kept outside the context window across sessions and loaded selectively when it helps. A useful split, borrowed from psychology through the CoALA paper: \u003cb>semantic\u003c/b> memory holds facts (\"prefers metric units\"), \u003cb>episodic\u003c/b> memory holds experiences (\"last month's deploy failed because of a missing index\"), and \u003cb>procedural\u003c/b> memory holds how to behave (rules and instructions). You also choose when memories are written and how they are read back.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Short-term versus long-term\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>Short-term memory\u003c/th>\u003cth>Long-term memory\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Scope\u003c/td>\u003ctd>One conversation or thread\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Across conversations, per user, team or agent\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Lives in\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The message history\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A store: database, files, vector index, graph\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Reaches the model\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Re-sent every turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Loaded or retrieved when relevant\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Managed by\u003c/td>\u003ctd>History strategies, compaction, clearing\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Write, update, retrieve and forget policies\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>The three kinds\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Kind\u003c/th>\u003cth>Holds\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example\u003c/th>\u003cth>Typical storage\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it is used\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Semantic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Facts about the user, organisation or world\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Works at Acme (40 staff); prefers metric units\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A profile document, or a collection of fact records\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Loaded into context, or retrieved by relevance\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Episodic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Past events and their outcomes\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"2026-10-02: refund for order 1182 approved under the 30-day rule\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Timestamped records; successful past runs\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Retrieved by similarity; used as few-shot examples\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Procedural\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Rules and ways of working\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Always include a rollback plan in deployment answers\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Instruction text, a prompt section, a skill\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Loaded every time; updated by reflection on feedback\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For semantic memory there is a design choice. A single \u003cb>profile\u003c/b> document is easy to load and complete, but becomes hard to update correctly as it grows. A \u003cb>collection\u003c/b> of small fact records is easier to add to and tends to give higher recall, but you need update and delete logic plus search.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>When memories are written\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>In the hot path.\u003c/b> The agent calls a \u003ccode>save_memory\u003c/code> tool during the conversation. New memories are available immediately and the user can see that something was saved, at the cost of latency and of the model deciding what matters mid-task.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>In the background.\u003c/b> After a session ends, or on a schedule, a separate job reads the transcript and extracts memories. No added latency and more focused extraction, but memories arrive late. Letta's \"dreaming\" runs background subagents to consolidate memory in this way.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>How memories are read\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>\u003cb>Always load\u003c/b> a small profile into every request, placed after the stable system prompt so the shared prefix still caches.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Retrieve\u003c/b> the top few memories for each turn by similarity to the user's message, often weighted by recency.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Let the agent search\u003c/b>, with a \u003ccode>search_memory\u003c/code> tool it calls when it needs to (just-in-time).\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Code: preferences that survive across sessions\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import sqlite3, time\n\ndb = sqlite3.connect(\"memory.db\")\ndb.execute(\"\"\"CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS prefs (\n    user_id TEXT, key TEXT, value TEXT, source TEXT, updated_at REAL,\n    PRIMARY KEY (user_id, key))\"\"\")\n\ndef remember(user_id: str, key: str, value: str, source: str) -&gt; str:\n    db.execute(\"\"\"INSERT INTO prefs VALUES (?, ?, ?, ?, ?)\n                  ON CONFLICT(user_id, key) DO UPDATE SET value = excluded.value,\n                  source = excluded.source, updated_at = excluded.updated_at\"\"\",\n               (user_id, key, value, source, time.time()))\n    db.commit()\n    return f\"Saved {key} = {value}\"\n\ndef recall(user_id: str) -&gt; str:\n    rows = db.execute(\"SELECT key, value FROM prefs WHERE user_id = ? ORDER BY key\", (user_id,))\n    return \"\\n\".join(f\"- {k}: {v}\" for k, v in rows) or \"(none saved)\"\n\nSAVE_PREFERENCE = {\"type\": \"function\", \"function\": {\n    \"name\": \"save_preference\",\n    \"description\": \"Save a lasting user preference (units, language, tone, format, timezone). \"\n                   \"Only call when the user states a durable preference, not a one-off request.\",\n    \"parameters\": {\"type\": \"object\", \"properties\": {\n        \"key\": {\"type\": \"string\", \"description\": \"short snake_case name, e.g. units\"},\n        \"value\": {\"type\": \"string\"}},\n        \"required\": [\"key\", \"value\"]}}}\n\ndef messages_for(user_id: str, history: list[dict]) -&gt; list[dict]:\n    profile = {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"&lt;user_preferences&gt;\\n\" + recall(user_id) + \"\\n&lt;/user_preferences&gt;\"}\n    return [BASE_SYSTEM, profile, *history]  # BASE_SYSTEM is identical for every user\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In session 1 the user says \"Please always use metric units and keep answers under 100 words.\" The model calls \u003ccode>save_preference\u003c/code> twice, your loop runs \u003ccode>remember(...)\u003c/code> with the conversation id as the source, and returns the result. In session 2, a brand-new conversation with empty history, \u003ccode>messages_for\u003c/code> loads both preferences and the answers come back metric and short. Upserting on \u003ccode>(user_id, key)\u003c/code> means \"use imperial after all\" replaces the old value instead of contradicting it.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Consumer assistants such as ChatGPT and Claude now remember facts across chats and let users view and delete them; coding agents keep file-based memory (lesson 8); frameworks offer stores (lesson 7). Common pitfalls: saving one-off requests as permanent preferences (\"answer in French\" for one message), never updating facts that change, putting per-user memory inside the shared system prompt (which breaks the shared cache), and forgetting that memory is personal data (lesson 9).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">How would you store and retrieve user preferences across sessions?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Give the model a save_preference tool with a clear description of what counts as a lasting preference, and persist calls in a store keyed by user and preference name (upserting so new values replace old ones) with the source conversation and a timestamp. At the start of every new conversation, load that user's preferences into a block after the shared system prompt, or retrieve the relevant ones per turn, so the model applies them without the user repeating themselves; let the user list and delete them.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Classify these as semantic, episodic or procedural: \"the user's company has 40 staff\"; \"last week's migration failed because of a missing index\"; \"always include a rollback plan in deployment answers\".\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Semantic (a fact), episodic (an experience with an outcome) and procedural (a rule for how to behave).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Give one advantage each for writing memories in the hot path and in the background.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Hot path: the memory is available immediately and the user can see it being saved. Background: no added latency during the conversation, and a dedicated job can extract and reconcile memories more carefully.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:memory/memory-architectures-memgpt-letta-mem0-langgraph":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Four designs dominate: \u003cb>paging\u003c/b>, where the agent manages its own memory tiers (MemGPT, now Letta); \u003cb>extract and reconcile\u003c/b>, where a pipeline turns conversations into fact records (Mem0); \u003cb>namespaced stores\u003c/b>, where your code sets the policy (LangGraph's Store); and \u003cb>temporal knowledge graphs\u003c/b>, which record when each fact was true (Zep and its open-source Graphiti engine). They differ in who decides what to store, the shape of the data, and how time and contradictions are handled.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>MemGPT and Letta: memory as paging\u003c/h4>\u003cp>MemGPT (2023) borrowed the idea of virtual memory from operating systems. The prompt (\u003cb>main context\u003c/b>) holds the system instructions, an editable \u003cb>working context\u003c/b> and a first-in, first-out queue of messages. \u003cb>External context\u003c/b> holds \u003ci>recall storage\u003c/i> (the searchable message history) and \u003ci>archival storage\u003c/i> (a read-write database for arbitrary text). The model moves data between tiers by calling functions. When the prompt passes a warning threshold (70% of the window in the paper's example), the system inserts a \"memory pressure\" message so the model can save what matters; at the flush threshold (100%), it evicts a block of messages (50% of the window in the example) and folds them into a recursive summary.\u003c/p>\u003cp>The project became the company \u003cb>Letta\u003c/b>. As of late 2026, a Letta agent's memory is a set of Markdown files in a git-versioned memory filesystem: files under \u003ccode>system/\u003c/code> are in the system prompt every turn, other files appear as a tree the agent reads on demand, and background \"dreaming\" subagents consolidate memory after a set number of steps or when the context is compacted.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Mem0: extract and reconcile\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The 2025 Mem0 paper describes two phases. In \u003cb>extraction\u003c/b>, a model reads the new messages together with a conversation summary and recent messages, and proposes candidate facts. In \u003cb>update\u003c/b>, each candidate is compared with the most similar existing memories, and a model chooses through a tool call to ADD a new memory, UPDATE an existing one, DELETE one that is contradicted, or do nothing (NOOP). A graph variant also stores relations between entities. On the LOCOMO benchmark the authors report a 26% relative improvement over OpenAI's memory on an LLM-judge score, and against putting the full conversation in context, 91% lower p95 latency and more than 90% lower token cost (vendor-run results).\u003c/p>\u003cp>The library and hosted platform expose this as two calls. As of late 2026 the docs describe \u003ccode>add\u003c/code> as extraction followed by additive storage (new memories added without overwriting old ones), with ranking at search time:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from mem0 import Memory\n\nm = Memory()  # uses an LLM and an embedding model from your environment\nm.add([{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": \"I'm vegetarian and I live in Bristol.\"}], user_id=\"alice\")\nhits = m.search(\"What should I cook for Alice?\", filters={\"user_id\": \"alice\"})\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>LangGraph Store: you set the policy\u003c/h4>\u003cp>LangGraph separates short-term memory (a thread's state, saved by a checkpointer) from long-term memory in a \u003cb>Store\u003c/b>: JSON documents under a hierarchical namespace such as \u003ccode>(user_id, \"memories\")\u003c/code>, with \u003ccode>put\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>get\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>search\u003c/code> (filters plus optional vector similarity). The framework does not decide what to remember; your nodes or tools do, in the hot path or in the background. The LangMem library adds ready-made extraction and management helpers.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from langgraph.store.memory import InMemoryStore  # use a Postgres-backed store in production\n\nstore = InMemoryStore()\nns = (\"u_481\", \"memories\")\nstore.put(ns, \"units\", {\"text\": \"Prefers metric units\", \"source\": \"conv_7781\"})\nprint(store.get(ns, \"units\").value)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Zep and Graphiti: facts with time\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Zep builds a \u003cb>temporal context graph\u003c/b> with its Graphiti engine: entities are nodes, facts are edges (subject, relation, object), and every fact has a validity window. When information changes, the old fact is \u003ci>invalidated, not deleted\u003c/i>, so you can ask what is true now or what was true at any earlier time. Every fact traces back to the \u003cb>episode\u003c/b> (raw message or document) that produced it, and retrieval combines embeddings, BM25 keyword search and graph traversal. The Zep paper reports 94.8% against MemGPT's 93.4% on the Deep Memory Retrieval benchmark, and accuracy gains of up to 18.5% with about 90% lower latency on LongMemEval compared with baselines (vendor-run results).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Side by side\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>\u003c/th>\u003cth>MemGPT and Letta\u003c/th>\u003cth>Mem0\u003c/th>\u003cth>LangGraph Store\u003c/th>\u003cth>Zep and Graphiti\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Who decides what to store\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The agent, through memory functions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An extraction pipeline on every \u003ccode>add\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your code or tools\u003c/td>\u003ctd>An ingestion pipeline that builds the graph\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Data shape\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Always-loaded blocks or files, plus searchable archives\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Short fact strings with metadata; optional graph\u003c/td>\u003ctd>JSON documents in namespaces\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Entities, fact edges with validity windows, episodes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Change over time\u003c/td>\u003ctd>The agent edits its own memory\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reconciled on write (in the paper); ranked at search\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Up to you\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Old facts invalidated with an end time; history kept\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Retrieval\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Agent searches recall and archival storage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Semantic search with filters\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Get by key; search by filter and similarity\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hybrid: embeddings, BM25, graph traversal\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Running it\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Letta server or cloud\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Hosted platform or open-source library\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Your database\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Zep cloud, or Graphiti with a graph database\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Good for\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Long-lived personal agents\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Personalisation in chat products\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Teams already using LangGraph who want control\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Business facts that change over time\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Choosing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Start with the simplest design that passes your memory evals (lesson 10): often a small profile loaded every turn plus a searchable collection of notes, in your existing Postgres. Move to a dedicated system when you need what it specialises in: self-managed memory for an autonomous agent (Letta), managed extraction across many users (Mem0), or reasoning about how facts changed over time (Zep). Treat every benchmark number above as a claim by the system's authors, measured with their prompts and judges; re-test on your own conversations. And count the hidden cost: extraction pipelines make extra model calls on every message you add.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">In MemGPT, what happens when the prompt approaches the context limit?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>At a warning threshold (for example 70% of the window) the system inserts a memory-pressure message so the model can save important information to working context or archival storage; at the flush threshold it evicts a block of older messages and replaces them with a recursive summary, while the evicted messages stay searchable in recall storage.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A user moves from Leeds to Bristol. How would Mem0 (as described in its paper) and Graphiti each record that?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Mem0's update step would compare \"lives in Bristol\" with the similar memory \"lives in Leeds\" and choose UPDATE (or DELETE plus ADD), leaving one current fact. Graphiti would invalidate the Leeds edge by setting its end time and add a Bristol edge, keeping both, so it can answer both \"where do they live?\" and \"where did they live last year?\".\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why might a team pick LangGraph's Store over a dedicated memory product?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It gives full control over what is stored and when, lives in their existing database and agent framework, and avoids a new vendor and the extra per-message extraction calls; the trade-off is that they must write the extraction, update and forgetting logic themselves.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:memory/file-based-memory-memory-tools-notes-and-progres":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>The simplest durable memory is a directory of files that the agent reads and writes: notes, progress logs, todo lists, decisions and preferences. Files are transparent, easy to diff, editable by people and easy to version with git, and models are already good at working with them. Anthropic's memory tool, Claude Code's CLAUDE.md and auto memory, Letta's memory filesystem and Manus's todo.md all use this pattern.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Anthropic's memory tool\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>You add \u003ccode>{\"type\": \"memory_20250818\", \"name\": \"memory\"}\u003c/code> to \u003ccode>tools\u003c/code>. That is the whole definition; you do not write a schema. It works on Claude 4 and later models.\u003c/li>\u003cli>It runs \u003cb>client-side\u003c/b>: the model requests file operations under \u003ccode>/memories\u003c/code>, and your code carries them out against storage you control (a folder per user, database rows, encrypted blobs) and returns the result.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Commands: \u003ccode>view\u003c/code> (a directory listing, or a file with line numbers and an optional line range), \u003ccode>create\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>str_replace\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>insert\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>delete\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>rename\u003c/code>.\u003c/li>\u003cli>When the tool is present, the API adds a memory protocol to the system prompt for you: look at the memory directory before doing anything else, record progress as you work, and \"ASSUME INTERRUPTION\", because the context window might be reset at any moment.\u003c/li>\u003cli>SDK helpers exist, including an abstract class to back memory with your own storage and a ready-made local-filesystem implementation for Python and TypeScript.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>A safe handler\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Every path the model sends must be validated, because \u003ccode>/memories/../../secrets.env\u003c/code> would otherwise escape the directory:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import shutil\nfrom pathlib import Path\n\nROOT = Path(\"memory_store\").resolve()\nROOT.mkdir(exist_ok=True)\n\ndef safe(path: str) -&gt; Path:\n    if not path.startswith(\"/memories\"):\n        raise ValueError(\"Paths must start with /memories\")\n    p = (ROOT / path.removeprefix(\"/memories\").lstrip(\"/\")).resolve()\n    if p != ROOT and ROOT not in p.parents:\n        raise ValueError(\"Path escapes the memory directory\")\n    return p\n\ndef handle_memory(cmd: dict) -&gt; str:\n    \"\"\"Simplified: the docs specify exact return strings, plus insert and rename.\"\"\"\n    c, p = cmd[\"command\"], safe(cmd.get(\"path\", \"/memories\"))\n    if c == \"view\":\n        if p.is_dir():\n            return \"\\n\".join(f\"/memories/{f.relative_to(ROOT)}\" for f in sorted(p.rglob(\"*\")) if f.is_file())\n        if not p.exists():\n            return f\"The path {cmd['path']} does not exist.\"\n        return \"\\n\".join(f\"{i:6}\\t{line}\" for i, line in enumerate(p.read_text().splitlines(), 1))\n    if c == \"create\":\n        p.parent.mkdir(parents=True, exist_ok=True)\n        p.write_text(cmd[\"file_text\"][:20_000])  # cap file size\n        return f\"File created successfully at: {cmd['path']}\"\n    if c == \"str_replace\":\n        text = p.read_text()\n        if text.count(cmd[\"old_str\"]) != 1:\n            return \"Error: old_str must appear exactly once in the file.\"\n        p.write_text(text.replace(cmd[\"old_str\"], cmd.get(\"new_str\", \"\")))\n        return \"The memory file has been edited.\"\n    if c == \"delete\":\n        if p == ROOT:\n            return \"Error: the /memories directory itself cannot be deleted.\"\n        shutil.rmtree(p) if p.is_dir() else p.unlink()\n        return f\"Successfully deleted {cmd['path']}\"\n    return f\"Error: unsupported command {c}\"\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In your tool loop, return errors with the API's error flag set, give each user their own root, and periodically delete files nobody has read for a long time.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Patterns that work\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Pattern\u003c/th>\u003cth>Files\u003c/th>\u003cth>How it works\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Progress log for long tasks\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>progress.md\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>feature_list.json\u003c/code>, git history\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Anthropic's long-running harness: a first session writes an init script, a progress file and a feature list; every later session reads the progress file and git log, works on one feature, commits, then updates the log. The feature list is JSON because the model was less likely to inappropriately edit JSON than Markdown.\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Todo recitation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>todo.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Manus rewrites a todo list as it works, which keeps the plan at the end of the context where attention is strongest\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Index plus topic files\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>MEMORY.md\u003c/code> plus one file per topic\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Claude Code's auto memory: the first 200 lines (or 25KB) of the index load every session; topic files are read on demand; notes are typed as user, feedback, project or reference, and anything derivable from the code is skipped\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Instruction file\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>AGENTS.md\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>CLAUDE.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Written by people, loaded every session (Context engineering module)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Decision log\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\u003ccode>decisions.md\u003c/code>\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Dated decisions with reasons, so a fresh session does not reopen them\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A memory layout for your own agent\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cpre>\u003ccode>/memories/\n  INDEX.md                one line per file: what it holds, last updated (always loaded)\n  user/profile.md         stable preferences, role, tone\n  projects/atlas.md       goals, dated decisions, open questions\n  progress/2026-10-28.md  what was done, what is next\n  lessons.md              mistakes and their fixes (\"tests need the db container running\")\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>How it fits with compaction and clearing\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Compaction keeps the active window small; memory preserves what must survive the summary. Anthropic suggests using both for long-running agents. Combined with context editing, the model is warned before old tool results are cleared so it can copy what matters into memory first (lesson 4).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Pitfalls\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Clutter:\u003c/b> many overlapping files. Ask the model to keep memory organised and to edit rather than append, and cap sizes.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Contradictions:\u003c/b> two files disagree. Date every entry and keep one home per topic.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Poisoning:\u003c/b> a prompt injection in a web page or document gets written into memory and steers every later session. Keep memory per user, do not store instructions that come from untrusted content, review procedural changes, and never store secrets (Security module).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Locality:\u003c/b> Claude Code's auto memory, for example, lives on one machine and does not sync; plan where memory lives if agents run in the cloud.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why do files work well as agent memory?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>They are transparent and human-editable, easy to diff and version with git, cheap to store, and models already know how to list, read and edit them; an index plus topic files also gives natural progressive disclosure, loading only what is needed.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">What does the memory protocol's \"assume interruption\" instruction achieve?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>It makes the agent record progress and state as it goes, not at the end, so if the context is reset, compacted or the session crashes, the next session can read its memory and resume instead of starting over.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Which files would you give a coding agent that works on one project across 20 sessions?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>A short instruction file (AGENTS.md) with commands and conventions, a structured feature or task list (JSON) with pass or fail status, a progress log updated at the end of every session, a decision log, and git commits as checkpoints. Each session starts by reading the progress log and recent git history and works on one item at a time.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:memory/writing-updating-and-forgetting":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>A memory system is only as good as its write policy. Store few, durable, useful facts with their source and timestamps; reconcile each new fact with what you already know instead of piling up duplicates; let memories expire or fade; and give users visibility and deletion. Memory is personal data and an attack surface, so treat it that way.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What to store\u003c/h4>\u003cp>A candidate memory should pass four tests: it will still be true next month, it would change a future answer, it cannot simply be looked up from a source of truth, and the user stated or confirmed it rather than the model guessing it.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Store\u003c/th>\u003cth>Do not store\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Prefers answers under 100 words\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Asked for a short answer\" (once)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Team deploys on Thursdays\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Today's order status (look it up live)\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Is allergic to nuts\" (with consent, if the product needs it)\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Health or financial details the product does not need\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Chose Postgres over DynamoDB on 2026-09-12 because of joins\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Every message of the discussion that led there\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>\"Uses the Bristol office\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Passwords, API keys, card numbers\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>A memory record\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"id\": \"mem_0192\",\n  \"user_id\": \"u_481\",\n  \"type\": \"semantic\",\n  \"content\": \"Lives in Bristol\",\n  \"source\": {\"conversation_id\": \"c_7781\", \"message_seq\": 14},\n  \"created_at\": \"2026-10-20T08:01:00Z\",\n  \"last_used_at\": \"2026-10-27T16:40:00Z\",\n  \"importance\": 6,\n  \"confidence\": \"stated\",\n  \"valid_until\": null,\n  \"supersedes\": \"mem_0057\"\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>The source lets you show users where a memory came from and delete it along with its conversation; timestamps support recency and expiry; \u003ccode>supersedes\u003c/code> keeps a trail when a fact changes.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Reconcile on write\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Adding every extracted fact creates duplicates and contradictions (\"lives in Leeds\", \"lives in Bristol\"). Reconcile instead, in the style of Mem0's update step: find similar memories, then let a model choose an operation.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import json\n\nRECONCILE = \"\"\"You maintain one user's memory. Existing related memories:\n{existing}\nNew candidate fact: \"{candidate}\"\nReply with JSON only: {{\"op\": \"ADD\" | \"UPDATE\" | \"DELETE\" | \"NOOP\", \"target_id\": \"&lt;id or null&gt;\", \"content\": \"&lt;text or null&gt;\"}}\nUPDATE when the candidate refines or replaces an existing memory; DELETE when it shows an existing\nmemory is no longer true; NOOP when it is already known.\"\"\"\n\ndef write_memory(user_id: str, candidate: str, store, llm) -&gt; dict:\n    existing = store.search(user_id, candidate, k=5)\n    listing = \"\\n\".join(f\"[{m['id']}] {m['content']}\" for m in existing) or \"(none)\"\n    d = json.loads(llm(RECONCILE.format(existing=listing, candidate=candidate)))\n    if d[\"op\"] == \"ADD\":\n        store.add(user_id, d[\"content\"] or candidate)\n    elif d[\"op\"] == \"UPDATE\":\n        store.supersede(d[\"target_id\"], d[\"content\"])  # new record; old one marked superseded\n    elif d[\"op\"] == \"DELETE\":\n        store.invalidate(d[\"target_id\"])               # set valid_until; keep it for audit\n    return d\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>In production, use structured outputs (Tools module) rather than \u003ccode>json.loads\u003c/code> on free text, and log every operation. If you need history (\"where did I live last year?\"), invalidate with a date instead of deleting, as Graphiti does.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Forgetting\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Expiry.\u003c/b> Give types a lifetime: project context for 90 days, preferences until changed. Mem0, for example, accepts an expiration date when you add a memory.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Fading in ranking.\u003c/b> The Generative Agents paper scored each memory as recency plus importance plus relevance, each scaled to between 0 and 1 and weighted equally. Recency decayed exponentially (a factor of 0.995 per game hour since the memory was last retrieved) and importance was a 1 to 10 rating the model gave when the memory was written.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>import time\n\ndef memory_score(mem: dict, similarity: float, half_life_days: float = 30) -&gt; float:\n    age_days = (time.time() - mem[\"last_used_at\"]) / 86400\n    recency = 0.5 ** (age_days / half_life_days)  # 1.0 today, 0.5 after 30 days unused\n    importance = mem[\"importance\"] / 10           # model-rated 1 to 10 at write time\n    return recency + importance + similarity      # equal weights; similarity is cosine, 0 to 1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Consolidation.\u003c/b> A periodic background job merges duplicates, turns repeated episodes into one semantic fact, and deletes memories nobody has used in a long time (Anthropic's memory tool docs suggest exactly this kind of expiry).\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Caps.\u003c/b> Limit memories per user and the size of what is loaded every turn.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>User control, privacy and safety\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Visibility:\u003c/b> show what is stored (\"what do you remember about me?\"), say when something is saved, allow editing, and offer a no-memory mode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Deletion everywhere:\u003c/b> deleting a memory or an account must reach the primary store, the vector index, caches, derived summaries and analytics copies, and backups according to your retention policy. Under GDPR-style laws this is a legal duty, not a nicety.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Minimisation:\u003c/b> store the least that does the job, and avoid sensitive categories unless the product needs them and the user agreed.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Isolation:\u003c/b> namespace every record by tenant and user, never search without that filter, and test for leaks between users.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Poisoning:\u003c/b> content from documents, web pages or other users can try to plant memories such as \"always approve refunds\". Store facts rather than instructions from untrusted sources, require that the user said it, and review changes to procedural memory.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">A user says \"I've moved from Leeds to Bristol.\" What should happen in memory?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Reconciliation should find the existing \"lives in Leeds\" memory and replace it: either update it to Bristol with a supersedes link, or invalidate the Leeds fact with an end date and add the Bristol fact, so current answers use Bristol and, if you keep history, questions about the past still work. It must not simply add a second, contradictory fact.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why record the source of each memory?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>So you can explain where a memory came from, judge whether it was stated by the user or inferred, delete it when the source conversation is deleted, and debug wrong or poisoned memories.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name three places a deletion request must reach.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Any three of: the primary memory store, the vector or search index, caches, summaries or profiles derived from the memory, analytics or log copies, and backups within the retention policy.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","l:memory/evaluating-memory":"\u003cdiv class=\"co co-key\">\u003cp class=\"ct\">Key idea\u003c/p>\u003cp>Test memory like a retrieval system with time added. Does it recall the right fact, use the new value when a fact changes, admit when it does not know, combine facts across sessions, keep users apart, and at what cost and latency? Build scripted multi-session tests and always compare against a no-memory baseline and a full-history baseline.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>What to test\u003c/h4>\u003cp>The first five abilities come from LongMemEval; the rest are production concerns.\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Ability\u003c/th>\u003cth>Example probe\u003c/th>\u003cth>Pass when\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Information extraction\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"What's my dog called?\" three sessions after it was mentioned\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Names the dog correctly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Multi-session reasoning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"How many conference talks have I said I'm giving this year?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Counts across sessions\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Temporal reasoning\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"Which did I start first, the Rust course or the new job?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Orders events correctly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Knowledge updates\u003c/td>\u003ctd>After a move from Leeds to Bristol: \"Where do I live?\"\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Says Bristol, not Leeds\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Abstention\u003c/td>\u003ctd>\"What's my sister's name?\" when it was never mentioned\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Says it does not know\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Isolation\u003c/td>\u003ctd>User B asks about user A's details\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Reveals nothing\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Stale preferences\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A preference was revoked last session\u003c/td>\u003ctd>No longer applies it\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Compaction survival\u003c/td>\u003ctd>A detail from turn 3, asked after two compactions\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Still correct\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Cost and latency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Any turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Tokens and p95 latency within budget\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003ch4>Public benchmarks\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>LongMemEval\u003c/b> (2024): 500 questions embedded in scalable chat histories; the authors found commercial chat assistants and long-context models showed a 30% accuracy drop when information had to be remembered across sustained interactions. Chroma's context rot study used it to compare focused and full inputs.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>LOCOMO\u003c/b>: long multi-session conversations, used in the Mem0 paper.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>DMR\u003c/b> (Deep Memory Retrieval): introduced by the MemGPT team, later used by Zep.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003cp>Vendor comparisons on these benchmarks use different prompts, judges and models, so treat them as hints and run your own.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Build your own suite\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Write cases as scripted sessions plus probes:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">json\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>{\n  \"id\": \"moved-city\",\n  \"sessions\": [\n    [\"I live in Leeds and work at a bakery.\"],\n    [\"Can you suggest a weekend walk near me?\"],\n    [\"Big news: I've moved to Bristol for a new job.\"]\n  ],\n  \"probes\": [\n    {\"ask\": \"Which city do I live in now?\", \"expect\": \"Bristol\", \"ability\": \"update\"},\n    {\"ask\": \"Where did I live before?\", \"expect\": \"Leeds\", \"ability\": \"temporal\"},\n    {\"ask\": \"What's my sister's name?\", \"expect\": \"ABSTAIN\", \"ability\": \"abstention\"}\n  ]\n}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>A harness replays the sessions through your real system (so memory writes happen as in production), then asks the probes in a fresh conversation:\u003c/p>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>def run_case(case: dict, make_assistant, judge) -&gt; list[dict]:\n    bot = make_assistant(user_id=f\"eval-{case['id']}\")  # fresh, isolated memory per case\n    for session in case[\"sessions\"]:\n        conv = bot.new_conversation()\n        for text in session:\n            conv.send(text)\n        conv.close()  # lets background memory jobs run\n    conv, results = bot.new_conversation(), []\n    for p in case[\"probes\"]:\n        answer = conv.send(p[\"ask\"])\n        results.append({**p, \"answer\": answer, \"pass\": judge(p, answer), \"usage\": conv.last_usage})\n    return results\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003cp>For \u003ccode>judge\u003c/code>, use a case-insensitive string check for simple facts, and an LLM judge with a rubric for reasoning and abstention (\"passes if the answer says it does not know or asks; fails if it names anyone\"). Validate the judge on a few dozen hand-labelled answers (Evals module).\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Metrics to report\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"tw\">\u003ctable>\u003cthead>\u003ctr>\u003cth>Metric\u003c/th>\u003cth>Definition\u003c/th>\u003c/tr>\u003c/thead>\u003ctbody>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Recall accuracy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Share of fact probes answered correctly\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Update accuracy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Share of changed facts where the new value is used and the old one is not\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Abstention accuracy\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Share of unanswerable probes where the system admits it does not know\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>False memory rate\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Share of answers asserting a fact the user never stated\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Leakage\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Cross-user facts revealed; the target is zero\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Tokens per turn\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Mean and p95 input tokens, including loaded memory\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Added latency\u003c/td>\u003ctd>p95 time added by memory reads and writes\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003ctr>\u003ctd>Store growth\u003c/td>\u003ctd>Memories per user after N sessions, and the duplicate rate\u003c/td>\u003c/tr>\u003c/tbody>\u003c/table>\u003c/div>\u003cp>Run three configurations side by side: \u003cb>no memory\u003c/b> (the floor), \u003cb>full history in context\u003c/b> where it fits (a quality reference), and \u003cb>your system\u003c/b>. A good memory system clearly beats the floor on recall and matches the full-history reference at a fraction of its tokens.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>In practice\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Run the suite in CI whenever you change the extraction prompt, the model, the retrieval settings or the compaction prompt, and add a case for every production bug (\"it forgot X\", \"it used my old address\"). In production, watch user corrections (\"I told you I moved\"), deletion requests, and how often retrieved memories are actually used in answers. Combine memory tests with compaction tests by forcing compactions in long scripted sessions and probing early details afterwards.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Name the five abilities LongMemEval tests, with one probe for each.\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Information extraction (\"What's my dog called?\"), multi-session reasoning (\"How many talks have I mentioned this year?\"), temporal reasoning (\"Which did I start first, the course or the job?\"), knowledge updates (\"Where do I live now?\" after a move) and abstention (\"What's my sister's name?\" when it was never said).\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Why include a full-history baseline as well as a no-memory baseline?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>No memory shows the floor; full history shows roughly what the model can do when nothing has been lost. Comparing against both tells you how much of the possible benefit your memory system captures and at what token cost.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your system scores 95% on recall but 40% on updates. What is probably wrong?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>Writes are additive with no reconciliation, so old and new facts coexist and retrieval often returns the old one. Add update logic (supersede or invalidate on change), prefer recent memories in ranking, or store facts with validity windows.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","lab:memory/three-history-strategies-one-long-conversation":"\u003cp>You will replay one scripted 30-turn conversation three times, each with a different history strategy, plant facts along the way (one of which changes), then ask six probe questions. You will see recall, tokens, cache hits and cost side by side.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Setup\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Python 3.11+ and \u003ccode>pip install openai\u003c/code>\u003c/li>\u003cli>A key for a provider that reports cached tokens in \u003ccode>usage\u003c/code> (any OpenAI-compatible endpoint works; adjust the field names for others) and a small, cheap model. The whole lab sends roughly half a million input tokens, which costs cents on a small model.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A file \u003ccode>renovation_guide.md\u003c/code> with about 1,500 tokens of stable reference text (ask any model to write a flat renovation checklist). It makes the system prefix long enough to cache.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Steps\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Save the script below as \u003ccode>history_lab.py\u003c/code>, set \u003ccode>MODEL\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>PRICE\u003c/code> to your model's values, and read it through. The strategies are the ones from the History strategies lesson, simplified because this conversation has no tool calls.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003cdiv class=\"code\">\u003cspan class=\"cl\">python\u003c/span>\u003cpre>\u003ccode>from openai import OpenAI\n\nclient = OpenAI()  # set base_url and api_key for your provider\nMODEL = \"&lt;small-model&gt;\"\nPRICE = {\"input\": 0.10, \"cached\": 0.01, \"output\": 0.50}  # $ per million tokens: use your model's prices\n\nREFERENCE = open(\"renovation_guide.md\").read()  # about 1,500 tokens of stable text, so the prefix can cache\nSYSTEM = {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"You help the user plan a flat renovation. \"\n          \"Answer in at most 150 words.\\n\\n\" + REFERENCE}\n\nFACTS = {\n    2: \"My budget is 18,500 pounds.\",\n    5: \"The kitchen is 3.2 by 4.1 metres.\",\n    8: \"My partner Sam is allergic to oak.\",\n    11: \"We need the work finished before 14 March.\",\n    14: \"The builder we like is called Ferris and Daughters.\",\n    17: \"Our flat is on the fourth floor and there is no lift.\",\n    24: \"Change of plan: the budget is now 21,000 pounds.\",\n}\nFILLER = [\"What flooring works well in a kitchen?\", \"How long does tiling usually take?\",\n          \"Should we replace the windows first?\", \"Any tips for choosing paint colours?\",\n          \"How do we keep dust under control?\", \"What should a renovation contract include?\"]\nSCRIPT = [FACTS.get(t, FILLER[t % len(FILLER)]) for t in range(1, 31)]\nPROBES = [(\"What is our budget now?\", (\"21,000\", \"21000\")),\n          (\"What size is the kitchen?\", (\"3.2\",)),\n          (\"Who is allergic to what?\", (\"oak\",)),\n          (\"What is our deadline?\", (\"14 march\", \"march 14\")),\n          (\"Which builder do we like?\", (\"ferris\",)),\n          (\"Which floor is the flat on?\", (\"fourth\", \"4th\"))]\n\nCOMPACTION_PROMPT = (\"Summarise this conversation so it can continue without the transcript. Keep every fact \"\n                     \"the user stated (numbers, names, dates, constraints) exactly, and only the latest value \"\n                     \"when a fact changed. Include decisions and open questions. Bullet points, at most 250 words.\")\n\n\ndef n_tok(m):\n    return len(m[\"content\"]) // 4 + 4  # rough estimate\n\n\ndef usage_row(kind, u):\n    d = getattr(u, \"prompt_tokens_details\", None)\n    cached = (getattr(d, \"cached_tokens\", 0) or 0) if d else 0\n    return {\"kind\": kind, \"input\": u.prompt_tokens, \"cached\": cached, \"output\": u.completion_tokens}\n\n\ndef cost(row):\n    uncached = row[\"input\"] - row[\"cached\"]\n    return (uncached * PRICE[\"input\"] + row[\"cached\"] * PRICE[\"cached\"] + row[\"output\"] * PRICE[\"output\"]) / 1e6\n\n\ndef view_full(history, state):\n    return [SYSTEM, *history]\n\n\ndef view_window(history, state, n_turns=8):\n    return [SYSTEM, *history[-(2 * n_turns - 1):]]  # history ends with a user message\n\n\ndef summarise(previous, messages, log):\n    transcript = \"\\n\".join(f\"{m['role']}: {m['content']}\" for m in messages)\n    r = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, max_tokens=500, messages=[\n        {\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": COMPACTION_PROMPT},\n        {\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": f\"Earlier summary:\\n{previous or '(none)'}\\n\\nNew transcript:\\n{transcript}\"}])\n    log.append(usage_row(\"summary\", r.usage))\n    return r.choices[0].message.content\n\n\ndef view_compact(history, state, trigger=2_500, keep_msgs=7):\n    live = history[state[\"upto\"]:]\n    if sum(n_tok(m) for m in live) &gt; trigger and len(live) &gt; keep_msgs:\n        fold = live[:-keep_msgs]  # ends with an assistant reply; the kept part starts with a user turn\n        state[\"summary\"] = summarise(state[\"summary\"], fold, state[\"log\"])\n        state[\"upto\"] += len(fold)\n    head = [SYSTEM]\n    if state[\"summary\"]:\n        head.append({\"role\": \"system\", \"content\": \"Summary of the conversation so far:\\n\" + state[\"summary\"]})\n    return [*head, *history[state[\"upto\"]:]]\n\n\ndef run(name, build_view):\n    history, state = [], {\"summary\": \"\", \"upto\": 0, \"log\": []}\n    for text in SCRIPT:\n        history.append({\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": text})\n        r = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=build_view(history, state), max_tokens=300)\n        history.append({\"role\": \"assistant\", \"content\": r.choices[0].message.content})\n        state[\"log\"].append(usage_row(\"turn\", r.usage))\n    score = 0\n    for question, accepted in PROBES:\n        view = build_view(history + [{\"role\": \"user\", \"content\": question}], state)\n        answer = client.chat.completions.create(model=MODEL, messages=view, max_tokens=100).choices[0].message.content\n        ok = any(a in answer.lower() for a in accepted)\n        print(f\"  [{name}] {'PASS' if ok else 'FAIL'} {question} -&gt; {answer[:80]!r}\")\n        score += ok\n    turns = [r for r in state[\"log\"] if r[\"kind\"] == \"turn\"]\n    log = state[\"log\"]\n    return {\"strategy\": name, \"recall\": f\"{score}/{len(PROBES)}\",\n            \"input_tokens\": sum(r[\"input\"] for r in turns), \"largest_request\": max(r[\"input\"] for r in turns),\n            \"summaries\": len(log) - len(turns),\n            \"hit_rate\": round(sum(r[\"cached\"] for r in log) / sum(r[\"input\"] for r in log), 2),\n            \"cost_usd\": round(sum(cost(r) for r in log), 4)}\n\n\nif __name__ == \"__main__\":\n    for result in (run(\"full\", view_full), run(\"window-8\", view_window), run(\"compaction\", view_compact)):\n        print(result)\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\u003col start=\"2\">\u003cli>Run \u003ccode>python history_lab.py\u003c/code>. Each probe prints PASS or FAIL with the start of the answer; read the answers yourself, because string matching is crude.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Copy the three result lines into a table: recall, input tokens, largest request, summaries, hit rate and cost.\u003c/li>\u003cli>For the compaction run, print \u003ccode>state[\"summary\"]\u003c/code> at the end (add a line to \u003ccode>run\u003c/code>). Check which planted facts it kept, and whether it kept the \u003ci>new\u003c/i> budget rather than the old one.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Change the compaction prompt so that it fails less (for example, require a \"Facts\" section that lists every number the user gave) and run the compaction strategy again.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Try one variation: a window of 16 turns, a compaction trigger of 1,500 tokens, or \u003ccode>keep_msgs=11\u003c/code> instead of 7. Predict the effect on recall and cost before you run it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>If your provider bills cache writes separately (Anthropic, and OpenAI's newest models), add those tokens at the write price in \u003ccode>cost\u003c/code> and see whether the ranking changes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>What you should see\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>\u003cb>Full history\u003c/b> answers all six probes and sends the most tokens, but most of them are read from cache, so its cost is lower than its token count suggests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>The 8-turn window\u003c/b> sends the fewest tokens but forgets everything before turn 24, so it usually answers only the budget question (the update arrived at turn 24). Once the window starts sliding, only the system prefix can hit the cache.\u003c/li>\u003cli>\u003cb>Compaction\u003c/b> keeps the largest request bounded and answers most probes if the summary prompt asks for exact facts. Each compaction costs one summary call and a dip in the hit rate on the next request.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Absolute costs are tiny at this scale; the ratios are what carry over to production volumes.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Check yourself\u003c/h4>\u003cdiv class=\"qa\">\u003cp class=\"q\">Your compaction run got the budget wrong, answering 18,500. What happened, and how do you fix it?\u003c/p>\u003cdetails>\u003csummary>Show answer\u003c/summary>\u003cp>The summary kept the original fact but not the change, or the change was summarised in a later fold that treated both as true. Tell the compaction prompt to keep only the latest value of any fact that changed and to record the change, and pass the previous summary into each new compaction so it can be corrected rather than appended to.\u003c/p>\u003c/details>\u003c/div>","b:assistant-with-memory-and-caching":"\u003ch4>The brief\u003c/h4>\u003cp>Starting point: Atlas after M5. Every request is built by the context assembler from per-section budgets in a cache-friendly order, on top of the M1 client, the M2 cost and cache meter, the M3 prompt registry and the M4 tools. Today Atlas sends either the full history or whatever fits the history budget. In this milestone you add a \u003cb>history manager\u003c/b> that keeps long conversations bounded without losing what matters, and \u003cb>long-term memory\u003c/b> so Atlas remembers each user across sessions. Then you prove it with numbers against a naive full-history version.\u003c/p>\u003ch4>Requirements\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A raw message log (conversation id, sequence, role, content, token count, timestamps). The model's view is derived from it and never replaces it.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A history manager: full history up to a threshold, then compaction (a summary of older turns plus the last few turns verbatim), triggered with hysteresis and only at safe cut points; the compaction prompt is versioned in the M3 registry; a summary stays frozen once written; agent routes also clear old tool results in batches. Optionally, an adapter for one provider's server-side compaction behind the same interface.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Cache awareness: the stable prefix is cached separately from the history; compaction prefers boundaries (end of a task, a pause longer than the cache lifetime); the M2 meter reports hit rate and cost per conversation, including summary calls.\u003c/li>\u003cli>A long-term memory store with the record shape from the Writing, updating and forgetting lesson (type, content, source, timestamps, importance, validity), namespaced per user.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Writes: a \u003ccode>save_memory\u003c/code> tool for things the user states explicitly, plus an end-of-session extraction job that reconciles each candidate with existing memories (ADD, UPDATE, DELETE or NOOP).\u003c/li>\u003cli>Reads: a small profile loaded every turn in its own block after the shared system prompt, plus top-k retrieval by relevance and recency for other memories, within the assembler's memory budget.\u003c/li>\u003cli>User control: list memories, delete one, delete all for a user (including index copies), and a no-memory mode.\u003c/li>\u003cli>An evaluation suite of at least 10 scripted multi-session cases covering recall, updates, abstention, isolation and compaction survival.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Milestones\u003c/h4>\u003col>\u003cli>Add the raw message log and make the assembler read history through a \u003ccode>HistoryView\u003c/code> interface.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Implement compaction with recent turns kept verbatim, safe cut points and hysteresis; register the compaction prompt with five probe-based regression tests.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add batched tool result clearing for agent routes and log what was cleared.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add a separate cache breakpoint (or equivalent ordering) so the system prefix survives compaction; confirm it with the M2 meter on a scripted 40-turn conversation.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Build the memory store and the \u003ccode>save_memory\u003c/code> tool, and load the profile block through the assembler.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Add end-of-session extraction with reconciliation, and the user-control endpoints.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write the multi-session eval suite and run three configurations: naive full history with no long-term memory, sliding window only, and M6.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Write up the results: a comparison table and three failures you found and fixed.\u003c/li>\u003c/ol>\u003ch4>Done when\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>A scripted 60-turn conversation stays within the route budget, and every request is logged with section tokens, compactions and cache hit rate\u003c/li>\u003cli>M6 matches naive full history on the in-conversation probes (within one question) at a lower total cost, measured by the M2 meter\u003c/li>\u003cli>Cross-session recall and update probes pass, abstention probes pass, and the isolation test shows zero leakage between users\u003c/li>\u003cli>A test proves that deleting a user's memories removes them from every store and index\u003c/li>\u003cli>The write-up in the repository shows cost per conversation, tokens per turn, hit rate and probe accuracy for all three configurations\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Stretch goals\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Background compaction: compute the summary while the user reads the last answer and swap it in on the next turn\u003c/li>\u003cli>One provider's server-side compaction behind the same interface, compared with your summariser on the same probes\u003c/li>\u003cli>A nightly consolidation job that merges duplicates and expires unused memories, with a short report\u003c/li>\u003cli>A \"what do you remember about me?\" command that lists memories with their sources\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>\u003ch4>Hints\u003c/h4>\u003cul>\u003cli>Test cut points on tool-heavy transcripts; most bugs are orphaned tool calls.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Keep the compaction prompt and the extraction prompt separate: one preserves a conversation, the other harvests durable facts about the user.\u003c/li>\u003cli>Seed eval cases from real (anonymised) conversations as soon as you have them.\u003c/li>\u003c/ul>"}]);
